'''''Lolium multiflorum''''' ('''Italian rye-grass''',<ref name="BSBI07">{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=2014-10-17 }}</ref> '''annual ryegrass''') is a [[ryegrass]] native to [[temperate]] [[Europe]], though its precise native range is unknown.<ref name="Quattrocchi2006">{{cite book |last=Quattrocchi |first=Umberto |title=CRC World Dictionary of Grasses: Common Names, Scientific Names, Eponyms, Synonyms, and Etymology - 3 Volume Set |publisher=CRC |year=2006 |pages=2408 |isbn=978-0-8493-1303-5}}</ref>

It is a [[Herbaceous plant|herbaceous]] [[Annual plant|annual]], [[Biennial plant|biennial]], or [[Perennial plant|perennial]] grass, depending on the environmental conditions. Italian ryegrass is grown for [[silage]], and as a [[cover crop]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Undersander |first1=Dennis |last2=Casler |first2=Michael| title=Ryegrass types for pasture and hay |work=Agronomy Advice |publisher=University of Wisconsin Extension |date=July 2014 |url=https://fyi.extension.wisc.edu/forage/ryegrass-types-for-pasture-and-hay/ |access-date=2025-07-10}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last=Moseley |first=G. |author2=E. L. Jones |author3=V. Ramanathan |title=The nutritional evaluation of Italian ryegrass cultivars fed as silage to sheep and cattle |journal=Grass and Forage Science |volume=43 |issue=3 |pages=291–295 |date=September 1988 |doi=10.1111/j.1365-2494.1988.tb02154.x |bibcode=1988GForS..43..291M }}</ref> It is also grown as an [[ornamental grass]]. It readily [[Naturalisation (biology)|naturalizes]] in temperate climates, and can become a [[noxious weed]] in arable areas and an [[invasive species]] in native [[habitat]]s.<ref name="Quattrocchi2006" /> Resistance to multiple herbicides, including those from the ESPS and ACcase groups, has been identified in wild populations of ''L. multiflorum.'' <ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Mahmood |first1=Khalid |last2=Mathiassen |first2=Solvejg K. |last3=Kristensen |first3=Michael |last4=Kudsk |first4=Per |date=2016-08-05 |title=Multiple Herbicide Resistance in Lolium multiflorum and Identification of Conserved Regulatory Elements of Herbicide Resistance Genes |journal=Frontiers in Plant Science |language=English |volume=7 |page=1160 |doi=10.3389/fpls.2016.01160 |doi-access=free |pmid=27547209 |pmc=4974277 |bibcode=2016FrPS....7.1160M |issn=1664-462X}}</ref>

It is a host plant to [[wheat yellow leaf virus]] and [[ryegrass mosaic virus]] in its native Europe.<ref name="Lapierre2004">{{cite book |editor1-last=Lapierre |editor1-first=Hervé |editor2-last=Signoret |editor2-first=Pierre A. |title=Viruses and Virus diseases of Poaceace (Gramineae) |publisher=[[Institut National de la Recherche Agronomique]] |year=2004 |location=France |pages=605 |isbn=978-2-7380-1088-9}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |doi=10.1111/j.1744-7348.1960.tb03559.x |title=The Transmission by Mites, Host-Range and Properties of Ryegrass Mosaic Virus |date=1960 |last1=Mulligan |first1=T. E. |journal=Annals of Applied Biology |volume=48 |issue=3 |pages=575–579 }}</ref>

It is sometimes considered a subspecies of [[lolium perenne|perennial ryegrass]] (''Lolium perenne''). It differs from ''L. perenne'' in its spikelet, which has a long bristle at the top, and its stem, which is round rather than folded.

It can be mistaken for [[elymus repens|couch]] (''Elymus repens''), which has spikelets along the broad side of the stem rather than the edge.<ref>''Reader's Digest Nature Lovers Library Field Guide To Wild Flowers Of Britain'', 1998, page 416</ref>

Other common names in English include '''Australian ryegrass''', '''short rotation ryegrass''', and '''Westerwolds ryegrass'''. It is also one of several species called '''darnel'''.

==Uses==
''Lolium multiflorum'' is widely used to provide large forage yields in short term leys where persistence of the crop is not a priority.
In the United States, ''Lolium multiflorum'' is sometimes used as a winter cover crop to prevent erosion, build soil structure and suppress weeds. As a palatable forage crop, it can be grazed by livestock and provide food in years when [[alfalfa]] suffers from winter kill.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.sare.org/Learning-Center/Books/Managing-Cover-Crops-Profitably-3rd-Edition/Text-Version/Nonlegume-Cover-Crops/Annual-Ryegrass |title=Annual Ryegrass |work=Sustainable Agriculture Research & Education |publisher=USDA |access-date=9 December 2015}}</ref>

